| Type of Tools (Repair) | Description |
|------------------------|-------------|
| Phone / communication | Used to gather symptoms from the user, guide them step-by-step (e.g. restart, safe mode, check cables), and confirm outcome. Clear questions and simple instructions reduce repeat visits. Document what the user reports and what was tried. |
| Remote desktop / remote support tool | Used when the user can boot and connect: view their screen, run diagnostics (event log, disk check, memory test), remove malware or bad drivers, and apply fixes without visiting. Requires user permission and network access; document all steps for the status report. |
| Knowledge base / diagnostic scripts | Used to look up error codes, beep codes, and known fixes; run scripted checks (e.g. disk SMART, memory test) to narrow the fault. Speeds up both online and on-site repair and ensures consistent procedure. |
| Screwdriver set and anti-static strap | Same as set-up and maintenance: open chassis, replace RAM, GPU, PSU, or drives. Anti-static strap when handling boards to avoid ESD damage. |
| Multimeter | Used to check PSU voltages (+3.3 V, +5 V, +12 V), continuity, and power at the connector. Confirms PSU fault before replacement and checks front-panel wiring. |
| Spare parts and USB installer | Known-good RAM, PSU, or disk for swap testing; USB bootable media for OS reinstall or recovery. Verify compatibility (socket, form factor, interface) before use; record serial numbers for the status report. |
| Diagnostic software | Memory test (e.g. Memtest86), disk SMART tools, event log viewer, temperature monitors. Run after repair to verify stability and document test result. |

