| Key Term | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| POST (Power-On Self-Test) | Firmware checks run at boot (RAM, drives, keyboard, etc.). Beep codes or on-screen codes indicate which check failed. Consult the motherboard or system manual for the meaning of codes. No beep or no display often points to PSU, motherboard, RAM, or CPU. |
| Safe mode / recovery | The OS loads with minimal drivers and services. Used to isolate software versus hardware faults and to remove malware or bad drivers. Recovery options (e.g. Windows Recovery Environment) allow restore point, repair, or reinstall. |
| Replacement parts | Use parts that are compatible (same form factor, socket, interface). Record serial numbers and warranty for the status report. Verify compatibility with the motherboard or system before ordering. |
| Computer status report | Includes fault description, actions taken (online and on-site), parts used, test result, and recommendation (e.g. follow-up, disposal, user training). Used for handover and warranty or asset records. |

**Online troubleshooting steps**

| Step | Action | Notes |
|------|--------|--------|
| 1. Gather symptoms | Ask what happened (error message, when it started, what changed). Ask if power/display/USB work. | Document user’s words; avoid jargon. |
| 2. Reproduce and narrow | Have user restart, try safe mode, or run a simple check (e.g. open Event Viewer, check disk). | If remote access is allowed, connect and run diagnostics. |
| 3. Check event log and basics | Review Application and System logs; check SMART if disk suspected; check startup programs and RAM usage. | Look for repeated errors; note error codes for knowledge base. |
| 4. Apply fix or escalate | Remove bad driver, run disk check, clear temp files, or suggest reinstall. If hardware fault or no progress, schedule on-site. | Record every step and outcome for the status report. |

![On-site repair toolkit / multimeter](images/repair-toolkit-multimeter.jpg)

**On-site repair procedure**

| Step | Action | Notes |
|------|--------|--------|
| 1. Confirm fault and environment | Power on; observe POST, beeps, display. Check cables, vents, and user environment (power, dust). | Compare with job order; note any new symptoms. |
| 2. Run diagnostics | POST and BIOS; memory test; disk SMART; event log and minidump if OS loads. | Isolate hardware vs software before opening chassis. |
| 3. Open and inspect | Power off, unplug; open chassis. Look for loose cables, bulging caps, dust, burnt smell. Reseat RAM, GPU, cables. | Use anti-static strap; document what you see. |
| 4. Swap test if needed | Replace PSU, RAM, or disk with known-good part one at a time; retest. | Record serial numbers of parts used. |
| 5. Replace or reinstall | Install replacement part or reinstall OS/drivers; apply updates. Verify compatibility and warranty. | |
| 6. Test and report | Full boot, run diagnostic software, stress test if required. Complete computer status report with fault, actions, parts, test result, recommendation. | |

![POST / beep codes reference](images/repair-post-beep.png)

| Common Fault | Possible Cause | Action |
|--------------|----------------|--------|
| No power / no POST | PSU fault, loose power cable, wrong front-panel wiring, or motherboard failure | Check cable and switch; verify front-panel header; test PSU; replace or escalate |
| No display | Loose or wrong cable, wrong input, GPU or RAM not seated, or faulty GPU | Check cable and input; reseat GPU and RAM; try onboard video |
| Blue screen / crash | Driver, RAM, or disk fault; overheating | Check event log and minidump; run memory and disk test; check temperatures |
| Slow or freezing | Malware, too many programs, low RAM, or failing disk | Scan for malware; check startup and RAM usage; check SMART and disk health |

